@FindHao I think we should reopen this issue. I can still reproduce it on current main after running the script multiple times and I think this is because the crash is flaky due to race condition. For each kernel, it looks like Inductor checks whether the worker pool is warmed up or not, and if it's not it will compile on main (no crash) instead of the forked worker (crash)

Two more reliable ways I found to repro the issue:
1. Bump the kernel count from 4 to something large like 10 (this increased my repro script crash rate from ~5% to ~95%)
2. Set "cfg.worker_start_method = "fork"" at the start of the repro script to force the fork pool (this had 100% crash rate for me)

I put up [#185870](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/185870) as a pytorch side fix

@eellison for your repro, we need to be past Triton 3.7.0 since https://github.com/triton-lang/triton/pull/9578 is not included in that version
